    "description": "Kurdish is a Northwest Iranian language tradition with substantial regional variation across Iran and neighboring countries. Kurmanji, Central Kurdish and Southern Kurdish provide a useful broad framework, but local speech and neighboring languages make the boundaries more complex.",
    "tags": ["Kurdish", "Iranian languages", "Kurmanji", "Sorani", "Southern Kurdish", "Regional variation"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T09:29:40Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T09:29:40Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Kurdish in Iran: Varieties and Regional Context | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Kurdish in Iran through Kurmanji, Sorani and Southern Kurdish, with attention to local names, neighboring languages and research limitations."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "family-and-diversity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A shared name and considerable variation",
        "content": "Kurdish belongs to the Northwest Iranian group, within the Indo-Iranian branch of the Indo-European language family. That classification connects it historically with other Iranian languages without making it a dialect of Persian. The University of Manchester's Dialects of Kurdish project emphasizes a continuum: neighboring speech varieties can share features even when broad labels suggest a clear dividing line. [s1]\n\nA common scholarly framework distinguishes Northern Kurdish, usually called Kurmanji; Central Kurdish, associated with the name Sorani; and Southern Kurdish. Encyclopaedia Iranica places Kurdish across western Iran and neighboring countries, with additional communities farther away. These categories help orient a reader, but they should not be mistaken for three internally uniform ways of speaking. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-picture",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Looking more closely at western Iran",
        "content": "Regional detail changes the picture considerably. Habib Borjian's study of Kermanshah province identifies Central Kurdish in its northwest and Southern Kurdish across much of the remainder. Within Southern Kurdish, names such as Kalhori, Sanjabi and Zangana can refer to varieties associated with traditional tribal groupings. Kermanshahi can mean Southern Kurdish broadly or, more specifically, speech in and around the city of Kermanshah. [s3]\n\nThis distinction between a city, a province and a linguistic variety is useful when reading cultural descriptions. A reference to “Kermanshahi Kurdish” needs context. Borjian also describes Kalhori across parts of Kermanshah, adjoining Ilam and Iraq, showing why a provincial boundary is not a reliable language boundary. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "neighbors-and-labels",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Language boundaries and cultural connections",
        "content": "The region includes languages that should not automatically be folded into Kurdish. Borjian distinguishes Kurdish, Gurani and Persian in Kermanshah's linguistic landscape. His account describes bilingual Kurdish–Persian settings and Gurani-speaking communities where multilingualism adds another layer. This makes a simple equation between residence, cultural affiliation and one language particularly misleading. [s3]\n\nSome labels remain disputed. The same study notes disagreement over the classification of Laki and explains that Feyli can create confusion because it has been applied to different linguistic varieties. Such cases call for explicit definitions rather than a definitive-looking map that conceals disagreement. For readers, the practical question is which community and variety a particular source is describing. [s3]\n\nManchester's project likewise treats neighboring Hawrami, Gurani and Zazaki as important to understanding the wider linguistic setting. Cultural proximity and linguistic classification answer related but different questions; neither should silently substitute for the other. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "documentation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How knowledge of Kurdish was assembled",
        "content": "The history of research helps explain why documentation is uneven. Joyce Blau's account traces European Kurdish studies to missionaries and identifies Maurizio Garzoni's grammar and vocabulary, published in 1787, as an early landmark. It also discusses later work around Urmia and Mahabad, including Ludvig Olsen Fossum's grammar of Mokri Kurdish, published in 1919. [s4]\n\nThese works document particular varieties through the purposes and locations of their authors; they are not interchangeable descriptions of every Kurdish speaker. Modern dialect research is valuable precisely because it makes variation visible. A useful introduction to Kurdish therefore begins with its Iranian linguistic affiliation, then asks where a variety is spoken, what its local name means and how the available evidence was collected. [s1][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      }
    ],
